From b29de3a78c45d79e594fb8be06310bb5d89310bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 11:25:07 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 47/48] ggml: share command timing and trace spans between
 backends

Metal's timing module held two things: how Metal stamps a command buffer,
and everything a backend reports through the timing procs that has nothing
to do with Metal: the callbacks and kept spans, the thread's tag, the node
classification (attention, mat-muls, recurrent state, KV writes), the
graph op counts and the span plumbing. A second backend would have had to
copy all of it.

ggml-base now holds that half as ggml-backend-timing. Each backend registry
gets its own state from ggml_backend_timing_new, so its callbacks, user data
and kept spans stay separate; the thread's tag is shared, since a host sets
the same tag on every backend. ggml-metal-timing keeps its interface and
only converts Metal's host clock, so Metal reports exactly what it did:
"mtl.graph_ops" comes from the "mtl" prefix, and the Unix clock is now
std::chrono::system_clock, which is CLOCK_REALTIME on macOS and Linux.
